WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:05.040
STEWART BRAND: Luigi asks: The issue of trust between
humans and autonomous systems is not a

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.360
new one.
what sort of features or AI interactions

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:16.470
might increase trust in both directions?
IAN MCEWAN: Well, it's a very good question, I really

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:23.070
don't know the answer. What would increase our
trust? I wrote a short story in order to

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:26.490
get going on this novel. It's a
fivefinger exercise. And it's written

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:35.070
from far in the future looking back when
artificial humans have become so

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:40.579
stitched into life. And they served on
juries, and they served in the military.

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:50.850
They had rights and responsibilities. And
gradually we went back to 19th century

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:57.239
anti-slavery laws, and using much of that
language, in order to say you cannot own

00:00:57.239 --> 00:01:02.129
such a person.
I mean owning another human being is, I

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:13.560
think, a bad in itself. And then it became
non, very non-pc, to ask someone when

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:20.189
you're sitting at dinner with some
strangers, to say: are you real? It'd be extremely

00:01:20.189 --> 00:01:29.610
rude. And my narrator in that short story
had lived that transition. That it

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:35.189
would be just as intrusive as if you
were to say it dinner loudly, "I hear

00:01:35.189 --> 00:01:40.549
you've got a colostomy bag. Bad luck."

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:46.140
To say of someone, "Are you real?" is sort of
physically intrusive. Because you've

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.170
already accepted that they are
conscious, so what should it matter?

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:56.430
And so it starts with a man who's

00:01:56.430 --> 00:02:02.759
with his girlfriend. They're making love
and he feels he has to ask her. And

00:02:02.759 --> 00:02:10.610
the story really is all about her answer.
And I I think coming back to this

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:17.780
question: That would then be, once we
passed that point of even not even

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:23.480
wanting, let alone daring, to ask,
then we would have reached the point of

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:29.800
total trust. BRAND: Oh, interesting.
MCEWAN: That would be the the meeting point.

